<%page args="profile_src"/>
## The path Anaconda looks for the preinstalled Flatpaks at.
##
## pyanaconda hardcodes file:///flatpak/repo (see rpm_ostree/flatpak_manager.py),
## so the installer's root gets a /flatpak symlink pointing at the copy that
## mkksiso puts at the ISO root. The repo itself cannot be shipped from inside
## the installer's root filesystem: it is an install source, and Anaconda mounts
## the media at /run/install/repo.
##
## Note the git history -- this file used to `cp -a` the repo into ${root} as
## well, which put it inside the squashfs where the kickstart would never find
## it. Templates reach the installer's root, not the ISO root; that is what
## mkksiso is for.
symlink /run/install/repo/flatpak /flatpak

## Which remote the installed refs end up pointing at.
##
## InstallFlatpaksTask deploys the refs from the local repo, then adds the
## remote named in [Payload] flatpak_remote, repoints every installed ref at it
## and removes the temporary "Anaconda" remote.
##
## Anaconda's default for that key is "fedora  oci+https://registry.
## fedoraproject.org": without this override every preinstalled app and runtime
## comes out with origin=fedora and `flatpak update` follows the Fedora OCI
## registry instead of Flathub. The installation itself still succeeds, so the
## damage only shows on the first update -- which is why it got through a round
## of testing unnoticed.
runcmd mkdir -p ${root}/etc/anaconda/conf.d
runcmd cp -a ${profile_src}/10-flatpak.conf ${root}/etc/anaconda/conf.d/10-flatpak.conf
